# Using freebayes to generate VCF file
freebayes -f ref.fasta dedup.bam > out.vcf 


# Filtering VCF file using bcftools
bcftools filter -i "QUAL>30 && INFO/DP>20 && INFO/AF> 0.40 && INFO/SAP>=0.50" out.vcf >filtered.vcf

# Annotation of VCF file using snpEff
SnpEff isn’t installed like a typical Linux package (apt install). You usually just download and unzip it, so it only exists in that folder.
If you want to run snpEff from anywhere, you must:
Add its path to your system’s $PATH environment variable
Move the .jar file and config where your system expects
or run command from same folder path 
/home/linux/slokavia/snpEff
Building custom genome 
If you want to check if your ref genome is avalilable 

nano snpEff.config

If its not there edit genome in exisying file

nano snpEff.config 
Register yor genome 

H37Rv.genome : Mycobacterium 
To use a .gbk (GenBank) file with SnpEff, you must place it in a specific folder that matches the genome ID defined in snpEff.config.
I have made a folder names in folder H37Rv in which i have stored genebank file
Building genome using gbk file
~/slokavia/snpEff/data/H37Rv/genes.gbk
java -jar snpEff.jar H37Rv filtered.vcf > annotated.vcf

# Compressing and indexing VCF file
bgzip annoted.vcf
tabidx -p vcf annoted.vcf.gz

#tb profiler
tb-profiler vcf_profile -v 2 --txt annoted.vcf.gz


